Supporting material showcased in isolation — figures, tables, diagrams, and framed boxes.

<section #eh-caption | `<caption>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `frameables` · eHTML `<caption>` · JATS `<caption>` · `content` `structured` → `<children>`

*No kwargs or boolean flags.*

Produced by: canonical `<caption>`.

</section>

<section #eh-diagram | `<diagram>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `frameables` · eHTML `<diagram>` · JATS `<(no direct JATS counterpart; exported as <fig specific-use="enscribe-dsl-<engine>"> with the verbatim source in <preformat preformat-type="<engine>-source">)>` · `content` `empty` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `engine` | format-word | `mermaid` `abc` | The diagram engine — the language that renders the body. |
| `caption` | kwarg |  | Optional caption text. |
| `src` | kwarg |  | An @id reference (#313 consumer wiring) that pulls a stored &lt;dataset&gt; declared in &lt;data&gt; and feeds its opaque bytes as the diagram's engine source — e.g. |

Produced by: canonical `<diagram>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code-block #code:eh-diagram-1>
<diagram mermaid>
graph LR
  A[Start] --> B{Decision}
  B -->|yes| C[OK]
  B -->|no| D[Stop]
</diagram>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-diagram-1>
<diagram mermaid>
graph LR
  A[Start] --> B{Decision}
  B -->|yes| C[OK]
  B -->|no| D[Stop]
</diagram>
</minipage>

*The `mermaid` format word selects the Mermaid engine. The handler
emits the same `&lt;pre class="mermaid" data-enscribe-dsl="mermaid"&gt;`
contract as the legacy `&lt;mermaid&gt;` shorthand expands to.*

<code-block #code:eh-diagram-2>
<diagram abc>
X:1
T:Scale
K:C
CDEFGABc
</diagram>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-diagram-2>
<diagram abc>
X:1
T:Scale
K:C
CDEFGABc
</diagram>
</minipage>

*The `abc` format word selects the abcjs engine.*

</section>

<section #eh-fig | `<fig>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `frameables` · eHTML `<fig>` · JATS `<fig>` · `content` `structured` → `<figcaption>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `src` | kwarg |  | URL of an image to embed. |
| `alt` | kwarg |  | Alt text for the generated &lt;img&gt; when src is present. |
| `align` | kwarg | `left` `right` `center` `full-width` | How the figure is positioned in the document flow. |
| `width` | kwarg |  | Suggested rendered width. |
| `type` | kwarg | `image` `table` `code` `equation` `diagram` `multi-part` `other` | Optional classification of the figure's content. |
| `caption` | kwarg |  | Optional caption text. |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default on | Whether this figure participates in the document-wide figure sequence. |
| `+border` / `-border` | flag | default off | The frameable surface. |

Produced by: canonical `<fig>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:eh-fig-1>
<fig src=elephant.jpg | An adult African elephant.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-fig-1>
<fig src=elephant.jpg | An adult African elephant.>
</minipage>

*The simplest case. The src kwarg generates the &lt;img&gt;; the pipe
content generates the figcaption. The alt text defaults to the
figcaption text when not specified explicitly. The eHTML
element is HTML-native &lt;figure&gt; (not the custom-element &lt;fig&gt;)
because the HTML rendering surface is the HTML5 native element
while the enscribe canonical name follows JATS's shorter &lt;fig&gt;.*

<code #code:eh-fig-2>
<figure src=elephant.jpg | An adult African elephant.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-fig-2>
<figure src=elephant.jpg | An adult African elephant.>
</minipage>

*`&lt;figure&gt;` is the authoring alias. The normalize-to-canonical gate rewrites the
tagname to `fig` early; the rendered output is the same.*

<code #code:eh-fig-3>
<fig #elephant src=elephant.jpg align=right alt="A photograph of an elephant" | An adult African elephant photographed in Tanzania.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-fig-3>
<fig #elephant src=elephant.jpg align=right alt="A photograph of an elephant" | An adult African elephant photographed in Tanzania.>
</minipage>

*The `id` enables cross-referencing with `&lt;ref @elephant&gt;` (or the
canonical `&lt;ref @fig:elephant&gt;` colon-prefix form). Numbered by
default; the figcaption gets a "Figure N." label span prepended.*

<code-block #code:eh-fig-4>
<fig #revenue-table type=table |
<table>
  <tr><th>Quarter</th><th>Revenue</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Q1</td><td>$100M</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Q2</td><td>$120M</td></tr>
</table>
Quarterly revenue for fiscal year 2024.
>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-fig-4>
<fig #revenue-table type=table |
<table>
  <tr><th>Quarter</th><th>Revenue</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Q1</td><td>$100M</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Q2</td><td>$120M</td></tr>
</table>
Quarterly revenue for fiscal year 2024.
>
</minipage>

*A figure without src. The content (a table) is preserved as-is;
the trailing line becomes the figcaption. Author convention is
to put the caption text on its own line at the end of the content.*

</section>

<section #eh-frame | `<frame>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `frameables` · eHTML `<frame>` · JATS `<boxed-text>` · `content` `structured` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `title` | kwarg |  | Optional title rendered at the top of the frame (the frameable title-top convention), as a title= kwarg or a &lt;title&gt; child tag. |
| `caption` | kwarg |  | Optional caption text rendered at the bottom of the frame (the frameable caption-bottom convention). |
| `type` | kwarg |  | Optional classification of what the frame contains (note, warning, tip, theorem-block, etc.), preserved as the data-frame-type attribute. |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default on | Whether this frame participates in the document-wide figure sequence. |
| `+border` / `-border` | flag | default on | The frameable surface. |

Produced by: canonical `<frame>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:eh-frame-1>
<frame | A short callout.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-frame-1>
<frame | A short callout.>
</minipage>

*The simplest case. The handler emits a &lt;figure&gt; wrapper (the vocab
html_output.element `frame` is only the lookup key for handler-strategy
entries — the handler controls the actual element). +border is default on
for &lt;frame&gt;, so the class appears automatically.*

<code-block #code:eh-frame-2>
<frame type=note title="Important" |
Make sure to read this carefully.
>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-frame-2>
<frame type=note title="Important" |
Make sure to read this carefully.
>
</minipage>

*With a title rendered at the top.*

<code-block #code:eh-frame-3>
<frame #fig:method-box caption="Workflow steps" |
1. Collect data.
2. Clean.
3. Model.
>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-frame-3>
<frame #fig:method-box caption="Workflow steps" |
1. Collect data.
2. Clean.
3. Model.
>
</minipage>

*Numbered by default (#272). Shares the figure counter with
&lt;fig&gt;/&lt;svg&gt;/&lt;mermaid&gt;/&lt;abc&gt;; use -numbered for an unnumbered frame.*

</section>

<section #eh-minipage | `<minipage>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `frameables` · eHTML `<minipage>` · JATS `<boxed-text>` · `content` `empty` → `<sealed-subdocument>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `title` | kwarg |  | Optional title rendered at the top of the minipage (the frameable title-top convention). |
| `caption` | kwarg |  | Optional caption rendered at the bottom of the minipage (the frameable caption-bottom convention), with the "Minipage N." label folded in when numbered. |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default on | Whether this minipage is numbered. |
| `+border` / `-border` | flag | default on | The frameable surface. |

Produced by: canonical `<minipage>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code #code:eh-minipage-1>
<minipage | Two panels side by side.>
</code>

<minipage #mp:eh-minipage-1>
<minipage | Two panels side by side.>
</minipage>

*The simplest case. The handler emits a &lt;figure&gt; wrapper (the vocab
html_output.element `minipage` is only the lookup key for handler-strategy
entries — the handler controls the actual element). +border is default on
for &lt;minipage&gt;, so the class appears automatically. The body renders as
sealed eHTML.*

<code-block #code:eh-minipage-2>
<minipage #mp:compare caption="Side-by-side comparison" |
A figure here counts privately.

<fig #fig:left src="left.png" | Left panel.>
<fig #fig:right src="right.png" | Right panel.>
>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-minipage-2>
<minipage #mp:compare caption="Side-by-side comparison" |
A figure here counts privately.

<fig #fig:left src="left.png" | Left panel.>
<fig #fig:right src="right.png" | Right panel.>
>
</minipage>

*Numbered by default (#272). It counts in its own
"Minipage N" series — `&lt;ref @mp:compare&gt;` resolves to "minipage 1". The two
inner figures number 1 and 2 in the minipage's PRIVATE figure counter, NOT
the document's: a document &lt;fig&gt; elsewhere is unaffected, and an outside
`&lt;ref @fig:left&gt;` is a normal not-found ref-error (the seal forbids inbound
references to the body).*

</section>

<section #eh-svg | `<svg>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `frameables` · eHTML `<svg>` · JATS `<graphic>` · `content` `empty` → `<raw-svg-source>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `width` | kwarg |  | SVG width attribute. |
| `height` | kwarg |  | SVG height attribute. |
| `viewBox` | kwarg |  | SVG viewBox attribute. |
| `caption` | kwarg |  | Optional caption text rendered in a sibling &lt;figcaption&gt;. |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default on | Whether this SVG participates in the document-wide figure sequence (shares the `figure` counter with &lt;fig&gt;, &lt;mermaid&gt;, &lt;abc&gt;). |
| `+border` / `-border` | flag | default off | The frameable surface. |

Produced by: canonical `<svg>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code-block #code:eh-svg-1>
<svg -numbered viewBox="0 0 100 100" width=200 height=200 |
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" fill="blue" />
>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-svg-1>
<svg -numbered viewBox="0 0 100 100" width=200 height=200 |
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" fill="blue" />
>
</minipage>

*A lone inline SVG. `&lt;svg&gt;` is numbered by default (it shares the
figure counter), so `-numbered` is what opts out of framing for a
purely inline graphic. The source is opaque pipe content; the
attributes pass through to the rendered &lt;svg&gt; element.*

<code-block #code:eh-svg-2>
<svg #fig:diagram viewBox="0 0 100 100" caption="A simple circle" |
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" />
>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-svg-2>
<svg #fig:diagram viewBox="0 0 100 100" caption="A simple circle" |
  <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="40" />
>
</minipage>

*Captioned and numbered → framed by the ordinary frameable rule: the
handler wraps the &lt;svg&gt; in a &lt;figure&gt; with the &lt;figcaption&gt; as a
sibling inside the wrapper (figcaption is not a valid child of &lt;svg&gt;).
Shares the figure counter with &lt;fig&gt;; `&lt;ref @fig:diagram&gt;` resolves
to "Figure N".*

</section>

<section #eh-table | `<table>`>

<sub-section | Specification>

`category` `frameables` · eHTML `<table>` · JATS `<table-wrap>` · `content` `empty` → `<children>`

| attribute | kind | values / default | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `format` | format-word | `csv` `tsv` `json` `yaml` `md` | Format of inline pipe content. |
| `parse-columns` | kwarg |  | #21: |
| `caption` | kwarg |  | Short-form caption as a kwarg string. |
| `src` | kwarg |  | Where the table's data comes from. |
| `type` | kwarg | `data` `layout` `comparison` `schedule` `results` `other` | Optional semantic classification. |
| `+headers` / `-headers` | flag | default on | Whether the first row of the data is a header row. |
| `+numbered` / `-numbered` | flag | default on | Whether this table is counted in the numbered table sequence and receives a "Table N." label prefix in its caption. |
| `+parse-text` / `-parse-text` | flag | default off | #21: |

Produced by: canonical `<table>`.

<sub-section | Examples>

<code-block #code:eh-table-1>
| Name | Price |
|------|-------|
| foo  | 1     |
| bar  | 2     |
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-table-1>
| Name | Price |
|------|-------|
| foo  | 1     |
| bar  | 2     |
</minipage>

*Plain markdown table syntax (via remark-gfm). The most common
authoring path for simple tables. No explicit enscribe tags needed.*

<code-block #code:eh-table-2>
<csv | name,price
foo,1
bar,2
>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-table-2>
<csv | name,price
foo,1
bar,2
>
</minipage>

*The `&lt;csv&gt;` shorthand lowers to `&lt;table csv&gt;` (the standalone `&lt;csv&gt;` /
`&lt;tsv&gt;` tags were retired to these gate shorthands) and renders today —
the table above is its output, parsed from the CSV source by the table
handler's csv engine. `&lt;csv | ... &gt;` and the qualifying form
`&lt;table csv | ... &gt;` converge to the same parsed table; only the authoring
shorthand differs. JSON data uses the qualifying `&lt;table json | ... &gt;` form
— there is no standalone `&lt;json&gt;` shorthand. See the `&lt;csv&gt;` vocabulary
entry for engine attributes (header control, alignment, etc.).*

<code-block #code:eh-table-3>
<table #revenue type=results>
  <caption | Quarterly revenue>
  <tr><th>Quarter</th><th>Revenue</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Q1</td><td>$100M</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Q2</td><td>$120M</td></tr>
</table>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:eh-table-3>
<table #revenue type=results>
  <caption | Quarterly revenue>
  <tr><th>Quarter</th><th>Revenue</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Q1</td><td>$100M</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Q2</td><td>$120M</td></tr>
</table>
</minipage>

*Explicit table with cells, used when fine control over structure
or attributes is needed.*

</section>
